          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for treating an acute migraine, and when is prevention considered?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could retrieve covers only part of your question, so this answer is incomplete.\n\n**Acute treatment:** The retrieved passage from a national headache society guidance summary says that treating on two or three separate attacks is the usual standard before judging a medicine ineffective, because attacks vary a lot. It also notes that non-oral routes are used when vomiting or slowed stomach emptying makes tablets unreliable. I could not retrieve the list of recommended acute medicines or the strength of the evidence behind them, so I can't tell you what that guidance recommends for first-line treatment.\n\n**When prevention is considered:** The same document says preventive treatment is offered when attacks are frequent or disabling enough that acute treatment alone is not enough. It gives the threshold as four or more migraine days a month with some disability. Fewer attacks can qualify if they are severely disabling, if acute treatment does not work or cannot be used, or if the person prefers prevention. The goal is fewer and milder attacks, not none. A 50 percent reduction in migraine days is the usual definition of a good response.\n\n**Evidence strength and disagreements:** The retrieved summary does not state how strong the evidence is for these points, and it does not flag any disagreement between authorities. I can't say whether other bodies set the same threshold. My search for disagreements failed when the tool budget ran out, so I have no verified information on that.\n\n**What to do next:** A clinician can talk through which acute and preventive options suit a particular pattern of headaches, including any other health conditions and medicines already in use. If you have a sudden, severe headache, or a headache with weakness, confusion, trouble speaking, or vision loss, seek emergency care now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
